update public.questions set solution = $F$Sum of interior angles $= (n - 2) \times 180°$. Hexagon: $4 \times 180 = 720°$. Quadrilateral: $2 \times 180 = 360°$. Difference: $720 - 360 = 360°$.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792402739721_jxc34a';
update public.questions set solution = $F$The radius is $0.7$ m. The curved edge is half a circumference: $\pi r = \frac{22}{7} \times 0.7 = 2.2$ m. Add the straight base of $1.4$ m: $2.2 + 1.4 = 3.6$ m.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792620230025_dswiqq';
update public.questions set solution = $F$'Mail' (letters and parcels) and 'male' (a boy or man) are homophones: they sound the same but are spelled differently and mean different things. Sentence D should say 'mail'. The other sentences use 'knight', 'night', 'wore', 'shone' and 'sea' correctly.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792046023044_7m14lt';
update public.questions set solution = $F$G is the 7th letter of the alphabet. $7 \times 2 = 14$, and the 14th letter is N.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792046214138_xx18yc';
update public.questions set solution = $F$Sentence C has a dependent clause ('Because the weather was sunny') and two independent clauses joined by a semicolon and 'however', so it is compound-complex rather than simply complex. A, B and D each have one independent clause plus dependent clauses ('After we finished our homework', 'who studied the hardest', 'that was baked yesterday'), so they are complex sentences.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792046088830_22prhm';
update public.questions set options = $F$["$2x + 3 = x + 9$", "$4x - 2 = 2x + 6$", "$5x = 3x + 10$", "$6x + 1 = 3x + 4$"]$F$::jsonb, answer_index = 2, solution = $F$Substitute $x = 5$ into each equation. A: $13 \neq 14$. B: $18 \neq 16$. C: $5(5) = 25$ and $3(5) + 10 = 25$ ✓. D: $31 \neq 19$. Only C works.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792374258196_q06msi';
update public.questions set options = $F$["$-2 + 7 = 5$", "$-2 - 7 = -5$", "$-2 + (-7) = -9$", "$-2 - (-7) = 5$"]$F$::jsonb, answer_index = 2, solution = $F$Moving left on the number line means moving in the negative direction, so moving left $7$ from $-2$ is $-2 + (-7) = -9$. A and D move right, and B has the wrong answer: $-2 - 7$ is $-9$, not $-5$.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792384370143_dek2hd';
update public.questions set options = $F$["$\\frac{45}{100}$", "$\\frac{9}{20}$", "$\\frac{5}{11}$", "$\\frac{45}{1000}$"]$F$::jsonb, answer_index = 2, solution = $F$A two-digit repeating decimal goes over $99$: $0.\overline{45} = \frac{45}{99}$. Simplify by dividing top and bottom by $9$: $\frac{5}{11}$. Check: $5 \div 11 = 0.4545\ldots$ Note that $\frac{9}{20} = 0.45$ exactly, which does not repeat.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792373905934_uj10ux';
update public.questions set options = $F$["$64.26$ cm²", "$46.26$ cm²", "$72.54$ cm²", "$54.86$ cm²"]$F$::jsonb, answer_index = 0, solution = $F$Square: $6 \times 6 = 36$ cm². Quadrant: $\frac{1}{4} \times 3.14 \times 6^2 = 28.26$ cm². Total: $36 + 28.26 = 64.26$ cm².$F$ where id = 'ai_1792374611383_geyyh4';
update public.questions set answer_index = 2, solution = $F$Area $= \pi r^2 = 3.14 \times 4^2 = 3.14 \times 16 = 50.24$ cm².$F$ where id = 'ai_1792620212013_46eyxn';
update public.questions set question = $F$A regular hexagon is rotated $120°$ clockwise about its centre. How many of these rotations does it take for every vertex to return to its starting position?$F$, options = $F$["$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$6$"]$F$::jsonb, answer_index = 1, solution = $F$Each rotation turns the hexagon $120°$, and a full turn is $360°$: $360 \div 120 = 3$. After $3$ rotations every vertex is back where it started.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792374396742_io1yys';
update public.questions set question = $F$A circular fountain has a diameter of $7$ m. Lights are placed every $2$ m around its edge. How many lights are needed? (Use $\pi \approx \frac{22}{7}$)$F$, options = $F$["$11$", "$12$", "$22$", "$7$"]$F$::jsonb, answer_index = 0, solution = $F$Circumference $= \pi d = \frac{22}{7} \times 7 = 22$ m. The lights go around a closed loop, so the number of lights equals the number of $2$ m gaps: $22 \div 2 = 11$.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792620230026_wkewhp';
update public.questions set options = $F$["A net with 5 rectangles arranged in a cross pattern", "A net of 6 rectangles: a row of 4, with one more rectangle above and one below the same rectangle in the row", "A net with 6 rectangles arranged in a straight line", "A net with 4 rectangles and 2 squares arranged randomly"]$F$::jsonb, answer_index = 1, solution = $F$A rectangular prism has $6$ rectangular faces. A row of $4$ rectangles wraps around the four sides, and the two extra rectangles fold up and down to make the top and bottom. Five rectangles is one face short, $6$ in a straight line can't close up, and faces placed randomly won't fold into a prism.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792374717060_wld6p8';
update public.questions set question = $F$Solve $9x + 3 = 4x + 23$.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792374258196_i0cdnf';
update public.questions set options = $F$["The movie was very exciting.", "The extremely runner completed the race.", "He speaks very loudly the words.", "The bright sun shines at the window."]$F$::jsonb, answer_index = 0, solution = $F$Adverbs such as 'very' can tell us how much of a quality something has. In 'The movie was very exciting', 'very' modifies the adjective 'exciting'. B uses 'extremely' with no adjective to modify, C has jumbled word order, and D has no adverb modifying an adjective.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792046062834_9yxe0r';
update public.questions set options = $F$["LIBRARY", "PRIMARY", "LIBERTY", "BINARY"]$F$::jsonb, answer_index = 0 where id = 'ai_1792046245175_e9pxcp';
update public.questions set options = $F$["RUNNER", "RUNWAY", "SPRINTER", "JOGGER"]$F$::jsonb, answer_index = 0, solution = $F$Reading P-R-U-N-N-E-R-J…, the letters R-U-N-N-E-R appear together: RUNNER, a person who moves quickly. The other words are not hidden in the sequence.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792380402745_nsold5';
update public.questions set options = $F$["Yes, the diagonals are definitely equal.", "Yes, the diagonals are definitely not equal.", "No, we cannot determine this from the statements.", "Only if the shape is also a rhombus."]$F$::jsonb, answer_index = 0, solution = $F$Statement (3) tells us the shape is a square, and every square has equal diagonals, so yes, they are definitely equal. Statement (2) is about rectangles in general, which no longer matters once we know the shape is a square.$F$ where id = 'ai_1792380491259_igmfax';
